{The "Opera Game": a model of rapid development and open lines.} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3
d6 3. d4 Bg4 $6 {Pinning the knight, but Black will have to give up the bishop
pair.} 4. dxe5 Bxf3 (4... dxe5 5. Qxd8+ Kxd8 6. Nxe5 {wins a pawn.}) 5. Qxf3
dxe5 6. Bc4 {Threatening Qxf7#.} Nf6 7. Qb3 $1 {A double attack on f7 and b7.}
Qe7 8. Nc3 {Morphy prefers development to material.} (8. Qxb7 Qb4+ 9. Qxb4 Bxb4+
{only trades queens.}) 8... c6 9. Bg5 b5 $2 {Black wants to chase the bishop, but opens lines towards his own king.}
(9... Nbd7 {is the healthy move:} 10. O-O-O {keeps the pressure on d7.} (10. Qxb7 $6 Qb4+
11. Qxb4 Bxb4 {[%csl Gb4] and Black is fine.}) 10... h6 11. Bh4 $14) 10. Nxb5 $1
{[%cal Gb5d6,Gc4f7] The knight sacrifice opens the c-file and the a4-e8 diagonal.} cxb5 11.
Bxb5+ Nbd7 12. O-O-O {[%csl Rd7][%cal Gd1d7,Gb5d7] All of White's pieces aim at d7.} Rd8
13. Rxd7 $1 Rxd7 14. Rd1 Qe6 15. Bxd7+ Nxd7 16. Qb8+ $3 {[%cal Gb3b8,Gd1d8] The famous
queen sacrifice.} Nxb8 17. Rd8# {[%csl Re8] A model of rapid development.}
1-0


